          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em variety of algebras
            is a classical notion from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"} universal algebra
            that subsumes nearly all of the usual kinds of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebraic
            objects, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            (over a fixed
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fields"} fields
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
          %p
            A variety of algebras is not to be confused with an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+variety"} algebraic variety
            %em every
            element satisfies the equations.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            A
            %strong signature
            (in this context) is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %strong operations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0, 1, 2, \ldots
            ) called its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arity"} arity
            %strong finitary
            if its set of operations is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
            (in the strictest sense for the purposes of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            = succeed ");" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arity+classes"} arity classes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            and a set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %strong variables
            = succeed "," do
              %sup#fnref:1
                %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            %strong word
            (in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            using
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tree"} tree
            in which each node is labelled by either a variable or an operation, such that every node labelled by a variable has no branches away from the root and every node labelled by an operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi o
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o
            has as many branches away from the root as the arity of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi o
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o
            %strong axiom
            (in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            using
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo =
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v = w
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi v
                    %mo =
                    %mi w
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash v = w
            %strong theory
            (in our sense) consists of a signature
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            and a set of axioms in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %strong finitary
            if its signature is finitary and its set of equational axioms is finite (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kuratowski-finite"} Kuratowski-finite
            in constructive mathematics); analogous remarks apply to other arity classes.
          %p
            A
            %strong variety of algebras
            is given precisely by a theory in this sense, but it is more usual to think of the variety as being the class (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %p
            Given a theory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %strong model
            or
            %strong algebra
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            consists of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            together with, for each operation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            with arity
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            from its
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+power"} cartesian power
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^\kappa
            such that, for each axiom
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi v
                  %mo =
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash v = w
            and each assignment of elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to the variables in that axiom, the equation holds that is given by applying the operations to the elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as indicated by the trees defining
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            (the meaning of which we hope is obvious). We say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %strong carries
            the operations and
            %strong satisfies
            the axioms.
          %p
            Given a algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            of the same (quasi)-algebraic theory, a
            %strong homomorphism
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            consists of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon A \to B
            such that, for each operation, the diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi A
                            %mi κ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi f
                                %mi κ
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi B
                            %mi n
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  A^\kappa   &amp; \stackrel{f^\kappa}\rightarrow &amp; B^n \\
                  \\downarrow &amp;                                &amp; \downarrow \\
                  A          &amp; \stackrel{f}\rightarrow        &amp; B
                  }
          %p
            commutes. We say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %strong preserves
            the operations.
          %p
            It is fairly straightforward to define a model (and homomorphisms) in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            in the two paragraphs above. But note that the operations and axioms still form sets, not objects of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is independent of the modelling category. In any case, given any theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            and any cartesian monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                variety of
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %h2#generalisations Generalisations
          %p
            A
            %strong sorted variety
            or
            %strong many-sorted variety
            is a generalisation. A
            %strong sorted signature
            or
            %strong many-sorted signature
            consists of a set, whose elements are called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong sorts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list"} list
            of
            %strong input
            sorts instead of merely a natural number (the length of the list) together with one
            %strong output
            sort. Each variable in an axiom comes equipped with a sort, which we may also classify as an output sort; then each branch in (the tree reprsenting) a word must link output sort to input sort. The root of a word gives the entire word a sort; the two words in an equational axiom must have the same sort. Then a model of a sorted variety consists of one set for each sort, and the rest should be obvious. A sorted variety is
            %strong finitary
            if it satisfies the finitarity conditions of a variety and its set of sorts is also finite. An ordinary variety as above may be called
            %strong unsorted
            or
            %strong single-sorted
            to avoid invoking the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/red+herring+principle"} red herring principle
          %p
            Another generalisation is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong quasivariety
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Horn+clause"} Horn clause
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong consequent
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong premises
            %strong finitary
            if this set of premises is (Kuratowski)-finite; a quasivariety is
            %strong finitary
            if it satisfies the fintarity conditions of a variety and additionally each Horn clause is finitary. An ordinary variety is a quasivariety in which each Horn clause has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
            of premises.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p For each definition of an appropriate algebraic object, there is a corresponding variety of algebras, a few of which are given below:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The variety of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
                is given by a theory with three operations
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo ,
                      %mi e
                      %mo ,
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m,e,i
                (traditionally called multiplication, identity, and inverse), with respective arities
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2,0,1
                (so multiplication is a binary operation, identity is a nullary operation, and inverse is a unary operation). There are five axioms, given by the following pairs of trees (with their traditional names):
              %ul
                %li
                  associative law:
                  #eq:ass.maruku-equation
                    %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
